1
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:39,960
CONVERSATION 1
You are a professor of physics at the University of Oxford.

2
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:44,680
You're a senior advisor at the European Organization for Nuclear Research.

3
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:49,200
You also seem to tour the globe tirelessly giving talks.

4
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:54,040
And in addition, you have your own weekly TV show on science.

5
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:55,700
Where do you get the energy?

6
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:59,420
Oh, well, I just love what I do.

7
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:04,220
I am extremely fortunate to have this life doing what I love doing.

8
00:01:04,220 --> 00:01:07,480
Professor, what exactly is your goal?

9
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:09,800
Why do you do all of this?

10
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:13,320
Well, as you said, I do have different things going on.

11
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:21,680
But these, I think, can be divided into two groups: the education of science and the further understanding of science.

12
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:25,160
Don't these two things get in the way of each other?

13
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:30,320
What I mean is, doesn't giving lectures take time away from the lab?

14
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:37,700
Not really, no. I love teaching, and I don't mind spending more time doing that now than in the past.

15
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:42,960
Also what I will say is that teaching a subject helps me comprehend it better myself.

16
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:54,560
I find that it furthers my own knowledge when I have to explain something clearly, when I have to aid others in understanding it, and when I have to answer questions about it.

17
00:01:54,560 --> 00:02:03,600
Teaching at a high level can be very stimulating for anyone, no matter how much expertise they may already have in the field they are instructing.

18
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:08,000
Any scientific breakthroughs that you see on the near horizon?

19
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,520
A significant discovery or invention we can expect soon?

20
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:18,400
The world is always conducting science and there are constantly new things being discovered.

21
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:23,160
In fact, right now we have too much data sitting in computers.

22
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:29,820
For example, we have thousands of photos of planet Mars taken by telescopes that nobody has ever seen.

23
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:44,280
We have them, yet nobody has had time to look at them with their own eyes, let alone analyze them.

24
00:02:44,280 --> 00:03:02,460
Q1. Why does the woman say she can be so energetic?

25
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:19,700
Q2. What has the woman been engaged in?

26
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:38,320
Q3. What does the woman say about the benefit teaching brings to her?

27
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:57,840
Q4. How does the woman say new scientific breakthroughs can be made possible?

28
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:00,640
CONVERSATION 2
Do you think dreams have special meanings?

29
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:03,060
No, I don't think they do.

30
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:04,240
Don't either.

31
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:05,840
But some people do.

32
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:11,400
I would say people who believe that dreams have meanings are superstitious, especially nowadays.

33
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:20,540
In the past, during the times of ancient Egypt, Greece or China, people used to believe that dreams could foresee the future.

34
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:28,500
But today, with all the scientific knowledge that we have, I think it's much harder to believe in these sorts of things.

35
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:33,880
My grandmother is superstitious, and she thinks dreams can predict the future.

36
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:40,420
Once she dreamed that the flight she was due to take the following day crashed, can you guess what she did?

37
00:04:40,420 --> 00:04:42,280
She didn't take that flight.

38
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:45,840
She didn't even bother to go to the airport the following day.

39
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:50,380
Instead she took the same flight but a week later, and everything was fine.

40
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:53,340
Of course, no plane ever crashed.

41
00:04:53,340 --> 00:04:54,580
How funny!

42
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:59,340
Did you know that flying is actually safer than any other mode of transport?

43
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:01,680
It's been statistically proven.

44
00:05:01,680 --> 00:05:03,700
People can be so irrational sometimes.

45
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:06,140
Yes, absolutely.

46
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:12,220
But even if we think they are ridiculous, emotions can be just as powerful as rational thinking.

47
00:05:12,220 --> 00:05:12,900
Exactly.

48
00:05:12,900 --> 00:05:17,180
People do all sorts of crazy things because of their irrational feelings.

49
00:05:17,180 --> 00:05:24,480
But in fact, some psychologists believe that our dreams are the result of our emotions and memories from that day.

50
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:33,860
I think it was Sigmund Freud who said that children's dreams were usually simple representations of their wishes, things they wished would happen.

51
00:05:33,860 --> 00:05:41,000
But in adults, dreams are much more complicated reflections of their more sophisticated sentiments.

52
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:48,820
Isn't it interesting how psychologists try to understand using the scientific method something as bizarre as dreams?

53
00:05:48,820 --> 00:06:00,420
Psychology is like the rational study of irrational feelings.

54
00:06:00,420 --> 00:06:18,080
Q5. What do both speakers think of dreams?

55
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:36,440
Q6. Why didn't the woman's grandmother take her scheduled flight?

56
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:54,220
Q7. What does the woman say about people's emotions?

57
00:06:54,220 --> 00:07:41,190
Q8. What did psychologist Sigmund Freud say about adults' dreams?

58
00:07:41,190 --> 00:07:52,830
PASSAGE 1
While some scientists explore the surface of Antarctica, others are learning more about a giant body of water four kilometres beneath the ice pack.

59
00:07:52,830 --> 00:08:00,210
Scientists first discovered Lake Vostok in the 1970s by using radio waves that penetrate the ice.

60
00:08:00,210 --> 00:08:07,010
Since then, they have used sound waves and even satellites to map this massive body of water.

61
00:08:07,010 --> 00:08:11,370
How does the water in Lake Vostok remain liquid beneath an ice sheet?

62
00:08:11,370 --> 00:08:22,390
"The thick glacier above acts like an insulating blanket and keeps the water from freezing," says Martin Siegert, a glaciologist from the University of Wales.

63
00:08:22,390 --> 00:08:28,430
In addition, geothermal heat from deep within the earth may warm the hidden lake.

64
00:08:28,430 --> 00:08:37,550
The scientists suspect that microorganisms may be living in Lake Vostok, closed off from the outside world for more than 2 million years.

65
00:08:37,550 --> 00:08:44,690
"Anything found there will be totally alien to what's on the surface of the earth," says Siegert.

66
00:08:44,690 --> 00:08:51,690
Scientists are trying to find a way to drill into the ice and draw water samples without causing contamination.

67
00:08:51,690 --> 00:08:55,090
Again, robots might be the solution.

68
00:08:55,090 --> 00:09:01,390
If all goes as planned, a drill-shaped robot will cut through the surface ice.

69
00:09:01,390 --> 00:09:09,830
When it reaches the lake, it will release another robot that can swim in the lake, take pictures, and look for signs of life.

70
00:09:09,830 --> 00:09:19,030
The scientists hope their discoveries will shed light on life in outer space, which might exist in similar dark and airless conditions.

71
00:09:19,030 --> 00:09:26,810
Recently, close-up pictures of Jupiter's moon Europa show signs of water beneath its icy surface.

72
00:09:26,810 --> 00:09:39,950
Once tested in Antarctica, robots could be sent to Europa to search for life there too.

73
00:09:39,950 --> 00:10:00,330
Q9. What did scientists first use to discover Lake Vostok in the 1970s?

74
00:10:00,330 --> 00:10:17,850
Q10. What do scientists think about Lake Vostok?

75
00:10:17,850 --> 00:10:33,530
Q11. What do the scientists hope their discoveries will do?

76
00:10:33,530 --> 00:10:48,210
PASSAGE 2
The idea to study the American Indian tribe Tarahumaras came to James Copeland in 1984, when he discovered that very little research had been done on their language.

77
00:10:48,210 --> 00:10:54,650
He contacted a tribe member through a social worker who worked with the tribesmen in Mexico.

78
00:10:54,650 --> 00:11:00,170
At first, the tribe member named Gonzalez was very reluctant to cooperate.

79
00:11:00,170 --> 00:11:04,190
He told Copeland that no amount of money could buy his language.

80
00:11:04,190 --> 00:11:13,290
But after Copeland explained to him what he intended to do with his research and how it would benefit the Tarahumaras, Gonzalez agreed to help.

81
00:11:13,290 --> 00:11:18,230
He took Copeland to his village and served as an intermediary.

82
00:11:18,230 --> 00:11:26,010
Copeland says, "Thanks to him, the Tarahumaras understood what our mission was and started trusting us."

83
00:11:26,010 --> 00:11:31,390
Entering the world of the Tarahumaras has been a laborious project for Copeland.

84
00:11:31,390 --> 00:11:34,370
To reach their homeland, he must drive 2.

85
00:11:34,370 --> 00:11:36,790
5 days from Houston, Texas.

86
00:11:36,790 --> 00:11:44,550
He loads up his vehicle with goods that the tribesmen can't easily get and gives the goods to them as a gesture of friendship.

87
00:11:44,550 --> 00:11:51,790
The Tarahumaras, who don't believe in accumulating wealth, take the food and share it among themselves.

88
00:11:51,790 --> 00:12:00,230
For Copeland, the experience has not only been academically satisfying, but also has enriched his life in several ways.

89
00:12:00,230 --> 00:12:11,390
"I see people rejecting technology and living a very hard, traditional life, which offers me another notion about the meaning of progress in the western tradition," he says.

90
00:12:11,390 --> 00:12:17,690
"I experience the simplicity of living in nature that I would otherwise only be able to read about.

91
00:12:17,690 --> 00:12:30,430
I see a lot of beauty in their sense of sharing and concern for each other."

92
00:12:30,430 --> 00:12:50,690
Q12. Why did James Copeland want to study the American Indian tribe Tarahumaras?

93
00:12:50,690 --> 00:13:09,010
Q13. How did Gonzalez help James Copeland?

94
00:13:09,010 --> 00:13:28,690
Q14. What does the speaker say about James Copeland's trip to the Tarahumaras' village?

95
00:13:28,690 --> 00:14:14,510
Q15. What impresses James Copeland about the Tarahumara tribe?

96
00:14:14,510 --> 00:14:16,790
RECORDING 1
What is a radical?

97
00:14:16,790 --> 00:14:23,790
It seems today that people are terrified of the term, particularly of having the label attached to them.

98
00:14:23,790 --> 00:14:43,730
Accusing individuals or groups of being radical often serves to silence them into submission, thereby maintaining the existing state of affairs, and more important, preserving the power of a select minority who are mostly wealthy white males in western society.

99
00:14:43,730 --> 00:14:47,190
Feminism is a perfect example of this phenomenon.

100
00:14:47,190 --> 00:14:56,710
The women's movement has been plagued by stereotypes, misrepresentations by the media, and accusations of man-hating and radicalism.

101
00:14:56,710 --> 00:15:04,610
When the basic foundation of feminism is simply that women deserve equal rights in all facets of life.

102
00:15:04,610 --> 00:15:15,050
When faced with a threat of being labeled radical, women back down from their worthy cause and consequently participate in their own oppression.

103
00:15:15,050 --> 00:15:23,370
It has gotten to the point that many women are afraid to call themselves feminists because of the stigma attached to the word.

104
00:15:23,370 --> 00:15:30,350
If people refuse to be controlled and intimidated by stigmas, the stigmas lose all their power.

105
00:15:30,350 --> 00:15:35,390
Without fear on which to feed, such stigmas can only die.

106
00:15:35,390 --> 00:15:44,150
To me, a radical is simply someone who rebels against the norm, who advocates a change in the existing state of affairs.

107
00:15:44,150 --> 00:15:53,310
On closer inspection, it becomes clear that the norm is constantly evolving and therefore is not a constant entity.

108
00:15:53,310 --> 00:16:04,490
So why is deviation from the present situation such a threat when the state of affairs itself is unstable and subject to relentless transformation?

109
00:16:04,490 --> 00:16:11,890
It all goes back to maintaining the power of those who have it and preventing the rise of those who don't.

110
00:16:11,890 --> 00:16:24,830
In fact, when we look at the word "radical" in a historical context, nearly every figure we now hold up as a hero was considered a radical in his or her time.

111
00:16:24,830 --> 00:16:27,690
Radicals are people who affect change.

112
00:16:27,690 --> 00:16:31,470
They are the people about whom history is written.

113
00:16:31,470 --> 00:16:33,890
Abolitionists were radicals.

114
00:16:33,890 --> 00:16:36,650
Civil rights activists were radicals.

115
00:16:36,650 --> 00:16:42,570
Even the founders of our country in their fight to win independence from England were radicals.

116
00:16:42,570 --> 00:16:53,170
Their presence in history has changed the way our society functions, mainly by shifting the balance of power that previously existed.

117
00:16:53,170 --> 00:17:03,350
There are some radicals who have made a negative impact on humanity, but undeniably, there would simply be no progress without radicals.

118
00:17:03,350 --> 00:17:17,450
That being said, next time someone calls me a radical, I will accept that label with pride.

119
00:17:17,450 --> 00:17:35,990
Q16. What usually happens when people are accused of being radical?

120
00:17:35,990 --> 00:17:52,670
Q17. What is the speaker's definition of a radical?

121
00:17:52,670 --> 00:18:12,270
Q18. What does the speaker think of most radicals in American history?

122
00:18:12,270 --> 00:18:17,350
RECORDING 2
We are very susceptible to the influence of the people around us.

123
00:18:17,350 --> 00:18:26,010
For instance, you may have known somebody who has gone overseas for a year or so and has returned with an accent perhaps.

124
00:18:26,010 --> 00:18:29,090
We become part of our immediate environment.

125
00:18:29,090 --> 00:18:32,870
None of us are immune to the influences of our own world.

126
00:18:32,870 --> 00:18:38,670
And let us not kid ourselves that we are untouched by the things and the people in our life.

127
00:18:38,670 --> 00:18:42,650
Fred goes off to his new job at a factory.

128
00:18:42,650 --> 00:18:47,810
Fred takes his 10-minute coffee break, but the other workers take a half an hour.

129
00:18:47,810 --> 00:18:51,030
Fred says, "What's the matter with you guys?"

130
00:18:51,030 --> 00:18:55,250
Two weeks later, Fred is taking 20-minute breaks.

131
00:18:55,250 --> 00:18:58,590
A month later, Fred takes his half hour.

132
00:18:58,590 --> 00:19:02,230
Fred is saying, "If you can't beat them, join them.

133
00:19:02,230 --> 00:19:04,730
Why should I work any harder than the next guy?"

134
00:19:04,730 --> 00:19:13,730
The fascinating thing about being human is that generally we are unaware that there are changes taking place in our mentality.

135
00:19:13,730 --> 00:19:18,530
It is like returning to the city smog after some weeks in the fresh air.

136
00:19:18,530 --> 00:19:24,270
Only then do we realize that we have become accustomed to the nasty smells.

137
00:19:24,270 --> 00:19:28,250
Mix with critical people and we learn to criticize.

138
00:19:28,250 --> 00:19:31,510
Mix with happy people and we learn about happiness.

139
00:19:31,510 --> 00:19:38,910
What this means is that we need to decide what we want from life and then choose our company accordingly.

140
00:19:38,910 --> 00:19:42,290
You may well say, "That is going to take some effort.

141
00:19:42,290 --> 00:19:44,030
It may not be comfortable.

142
00:19:44,030 --> 00:19:46,770
I may offend some of my present company."

143
00:19:46,770 --> 00:19:47,450
Right?

144
00:19:47,450 --> 00:19:49,870
But it is your life.

145
00:19:49,870 --> 00:19:53,530
Fred may say, "I'm always broke, frequently depressed.

146
00:19:53,530 --> 00:19:56,270
I'm going nowhere and I never do anything exciting."

147
00:19:56,270 --> 00:20:06,450
Then we discover that Fred's best friends are always broke, frequently depressed, going nowhere and wishing that life was more exciting.

148
00:20:06,450 --> 00:20:08,650
This is not coincidence.

149
00:20:08,650 --> 00:20:12,410
Nor is it our business to stand in judgment of Fred.

150
00:20:12,410 --> 00:20:21,690
However, if Fred ever wants to improve his quality of life, the first thing he'll need to do is recognize what has been going on all these years.

151
00:20:21,690 --> 00:20:30,030
It's no surprise that doctors as a profession suffer a lot of ill health because they spend their lives around sick people.

152
00:20:30,030 --> 00:20:36,010
Psychiatrists have a higher incidence of suicide in their profession for related reasons.

153
00:20:36,010 --> 00:20:41,710
Traditionally, nine out of ten children whose parents smoke smoke themselves.

154
00:20:41,710 --> 00:20:45,310
Obesity is in part an environmental problem.

155
00:20:45,310 --> 00:20:48,030
Successful people have successful friends.

156
00:20:48,030 --> 00:20:58,810
And so the story goes on.

157
00:20:58,810 --> 00:21:16,450
Q19. What does the speaker say about us as human beings?

158
00:21:16,450 --> 00:21:37,710
Q20. What does the speaker say Fred should do first to improve his quality of life?

159
00:21:37,710 --> 00:21:55,610
Q21. What does the speaker say about psychiatrists?

160
00:21:55,610 --> 00:21:59,810
RECORDING 3
Virtually every American can recognize a dollar bill at a mere glance.

161
00:21:59,810 --> 00:22:12,550
Many can identify it by its sound or texture, but few people indeed can accurately describe the world's most powerful, important currency.

162
00:22:12,550 --> 00:22:18,990
The American dollar bill is colored with black ink on one side and green on the other.

163
00:22:18,990 --> 00:22:24,590
The exact composition of the paper and ink is a closely guarded government secret.

164
00:22:24,590 --> 00:22:31,090
Despite its weighty importance, the dollar bill actually weighs little.

165
00:22:31,090 --> 00:22:36,830
It requires nearly 500 bills to tip the scales at a pound.

166
00:22:36,830 --> 00:22:43,450
Not only is the dollar bill lightweight, but it also has a brief lifespan.

167
00:22:43,450 --> 00:22:48,010
Few dollar bills survive longer than 18 months.

168
00:22:48,010 --> 00:22:56,390
The word "dollar" is taken from the German word "taler," the name for the world's most important currency in the 16th century.

169
00:22:56,390 --> 00:23:04,450
The taler was a silver coin first minted in 1518 under the reign of Charles V, Emperor of Germany.

170
00:23:04,450 --> 00:23:11,270
The concept of paper money is a relatively recent innovation in the history of American currency.

171
00:23:11,270 --> 00:23:19,950
When the Constitution was signed, people had little regard for paper money because of its steadily decreasing value during the colonial era.

172
00:23:19,950 --> 00:23:26,190
Because of this lack of faith, the new American government minted only coins for common currency.

173
00:23:26,190 --> 00:23:39,030
Interest-bearing bank notes were issued at the same time, but their purpose was limited to providing money for urgent government crises, such as American involvement in the War of 1812.

174
00:23:39,030 --> 00:23:48,410
The first noninterest-bearing paper currency was authorized by Congress in 1862, at the height of the Civil War.

175
00:23:48,410 --> 00:23:55,850
At this point, citizens' old fears of devalued paper currency had calmed, and the dollar bill was born.

176
00:23:55,850 --> 00:24:00,210
The new green-colored paper money quickly earned the nickname "greenback."

177
00:24:00,210 --> 00:24:10,710
Today, the American dollar bill is a product of the Federal Reserve and is issued from the 12 Federal Reserve banks around the United States.

178
00:24:10,710 --> 00:24:18,710
The government keeps a steady supply of approximately 2 billion bills in circulation at all times.

179
00:24:18,710 --> 00:24:23,230
Controversy continues to surround the true value of the dollar bill.

180
00:24:23,230 --> 00:24:31,130
American history has seen generations of politicians argue in favor of a gold standard for American currency.

181
00:24:31,130 --> 00:24:38,170
However, for the present, the American dollar bill holds the value that is printed on it and little more.

182
00:24:38,170 --> 00:24:54,690
The only other guarantee on the bill is a Federal Reserve pledge as a confirmation in the form of government securities.

183
00:24:54,690 --> 00:25:12,010
Q22. What does the speaker say about the American dollar bill?

184
00:25:12,010 --> 00:25:32,770
Q23. What does the speaker say about the exact composition of the American dollar bill?

185
00:25:32,770 --> 00:25:53,210
Q24. Why did the new American government mint only coins for common currency?

186
00:25:53,210 --> 00:26:16,101
Q25. What have generations of American politicians argued for?
